<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomPermission xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, DELIBERATELY.
        CustomPermission.Description is capped at 255 characters (measured 2026-08-12 via a
        failed dry-run deploy of this file's first draft: "Value too long for field:
        Description maximum length is: 255"). Broker_Protection_Access carries the same
        workaround for the identical reason (see its own header comment). A comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API, so it can be as long as it needs.

        SPIKE (2026-08-12) — proves whether a {!$Permission.X} FlexiPage Dynamic Actions
        visibility rule is honoured by the RENDERER, not merely deployed, at API 67.0 -
        see agent-output/design-requirements-custom-permissions.md section 7.1. This repo has
        measured a flexipage construct that deployed green, survived a retrieve, and was
        silently ignored by the renderer (Is_Decline_Allowed__c's parenthesised-OR finding), so
        a green deploy of this permission is NOT proof it works.

        It was NOT, at spike time, the layer-5 authorization token for the real Acquisitions
        deal-action gate - Apex still read User.Deal_Driver__c unchanged, and this permission
        was referenced by exactly ONE FlexiPage criterion (Development_Feasibility_Review_
        Record_Page). The spike PASSED (renderer honours {!$Permission.CustomPermission.X},
        confirmed by both a granted-persona and a non-granted-persona page load), which
        unblocked PHASE 1 below.

        🔬 PHASE 1 (2026-08-12) of retiring the driver-flag concept — see
        agent-output/design-requirements-custom-permissions.md. This IS NOW the layer-5
        authorization token the remaining 18 acquisition-gated flexipage criteria are
        repointed to (Opportunity_Record_Page x8, LOI_Record_Page x4, NDA_Record_Page x1,
        Contract_Review_Record_Page x1, Underwriting_Record_Page x2, Construction_Feasibility_
        Review_Record_Page x1 — plus the spike's original Dev_FR criterion, 19 total). It
        remains granted only through Acquisition_Deal_Driver, whose population is unchanged.
        UPDATED — a later developer pass swapped OpportunityActionPermissionService.cls off
        User.Deal_Driver__c entirely; it now calls
        FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Acquisition_Deal_Actions') as the sole authorization
        check, so this permission is the live server-side gate, not merely the chrome. The old
        two-mechanisms-agreeing description below is left in place, superseded, for history.
        User.Deal_Driver__c and its fieldPermissions grant are UNTOUCHED and remain the rollback
        lever; they govern nothing today and are deleted in a separate, later phase.
    -->
    <label>Acquisition Deal Actions</label>
    <description>Layer-5 authorization token for the Acquisition stage quick actions. Replaces User.Deal_Driver__c. Grant ONLY via Acquisition_Deal_Driver; never via a layer-4 capability set.</description>
    <isLicensed>false</isLicensed>
</CustomPermission>
